---
import Recording from "@/components/Recording.astro"
import CodeBlock from "@/components/CodeBlock.astro"
import DocsLayout from "@/layouts/DocsLayout.astro"
import { docsMetadata } from "@/lib/docs-meta"

const metadata = docsMetadata("/docs/cli/config")
---
<DocsLayout metadata={metadata}>
    <section>
      <h1 class="mt-0 text-4xl font-bold tracking-tight mb-4">gooze config</h1>
      <p class="gz-prose gz-prose--lead mb-8">You&apos;re setting up Gooze in a repo and want a config file to start from.{" "}<code>gooze config</code> manages configuration: <code>init</code> generates a starter config file, and{" "}<code>show</code> and <code>schema</code> (<code>v1.5.0+</code>) print the effective settings and the file&apos;s schema.</p>
      <div>
        <div id="usage">
          <h2>Usage</h2>
          <CodeBlock lang="bash" code="gooze config <subcommand>" />
          <p>Subcommands: <a href="#init"><code>init</code></a>,{" "}<a href="#show"><code>show</code></a> and <a href="#schema"><code>schema</code></a>.</p>
        </div>
        <div id="init">
          <h2>gooze config init</h2>
          <p>Creates a starter <code>gooze.yaml</code> in the current directory.</p>
          <h3>When to use it</h3>
          <ul>
            <li>First time setting up Gooze in a repo</li>
            <li>You want a known-good config file to edit</li>
          </ul>
          <h3>Usage</h3>
          <CodeBlock lang="bash" code="gooze config init" />
          <p>This writes <code>gooze.yaml</code> and stops.
            It does not run mutation testing.</p>
          <Recording name="config-init" title="Recorded gooze config init, run twice" />
          <p>It never overwrites an existing file: if <code>gooze.yaml</code> is already there it
            exits <code>1</code> with{" "}<code>gooze.yaml already exists; edit it or remove it first</code>. For every key the
            file can contain, see <a href="/docs/config/">Configuration (<code>gooze.yaml</code>)</a>.</p>
          <p>The file it writes holds every key in schema order with its default value and a
            comment that explains it. Environment variables and flags don&apos;t change it:</p>
          <Recording name="config-init-file" title="Recorded contents of the gooze.yaml written by gooze config init" />
          <h3>Next step</h3>
          <p>After you have a config file, run:</p>
          <CodeBlock lang="bash" code="gooze run ./..." />
        </div>
        <div id="show">
          <h2>gooze config show</h2>
          <p>Available in <code>v1.5.0+</code>. A value isn&apos;t what you expected, and you want to know which setting won. This command
            prints every config key with its effective value, where that value comes from
            (<code>flag</code>, <code>env</code>, <code>gooze.yaml</code> or <code>default</code>) and its environment variable.</p>
          <CodeBlock lang="bash" code="gooze config show" />
          <p>Only flags available to every command can show here. Flags of{" "}<code>gooze run</code> apply to <code>gooze run</code>.</p>
        </div>
        <div id="schema">
          <h2>gooze config schema</h2>
          <p>Available in <code>v1.5.0+</code>. Your editor can validate and complete <code>gooze.yaml</code> if it has the file&apos;s schema.
            This command prints it as JSON Schema, so save it and point your editor at the file,
            for example through <code>yaml-language-server</code>.</p>
          <CodeBlock lang="bash" code="gooze config schema > gooze.schema.json" />
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>
</DocsLayout>
